# Documentation + small results only. See analysis/README.md.
#
# DENY-ALL THEN PERMIT, BY EXTENSION. A new large file type is blocked by default rather
# than slipping into a commit. The cost of that safety:
#
#   *** A NEW FILE TYPE IS SILENTLY NOT COMMITTED UNTIL YOU ADD AN ALLOW LINE BELOW. ***
#   `git status` will not mention it. `git check-ignore -v <path>` names the rule that
#   caught it. If a figure or table "did not get committed", that is what happened.
#
# ANNOTATE WHAT YOU ARE *NOT* ALLOWING. The habit that makes this file useful over years
# is recording the size of what the deny-all holds back, so the next person can see the
# stakes instead of re-measuring. Once this directory has real content:
#
#   cd analysis && git status --porcelain --ignored | awk '$1=="!!"{print $2}' \
#     | xargs -r du -ch 2>/dev/null | tail -1                    # total held back
#   find . -type f | sed 's/.*\.//' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head   # by extension
#
# ...then keep a line here like:
#   # Deliberately NOT permitted (and large here): *.pkl (1.3 GB)  *.bam (400 MB)  *.pdf
*
!*/
!.gitignore

# --- documentation and code (always small) ---
!*.md
!*.qmd
!*.ipynb
!*.py
!*.R
!*.sh
!*.sbatch
!*.yaml
!*.yml

# --- results small enough to be worth committing ---
!*.webp
!*.tsv
!*.csv

# --- Quarto site assets (only present when use_quarto_site=y) ---
!*.css
!*.scss
!*.bib

# --- narrow, path-scoped exceptions go here, one per line, naming the producer ---
# e.g. !26_08_27_some_topic/gene_lists/*.txt   # produced by some_notebook.ipynb
#      Prefer a path-scoped exception over widening an extension globally.

# ...never these, whatever they contain.
# MUST STAY LAST: within a .gitignore the last matching rule wins, and `!*/` above
# re-includes every directory -- so these re-exclusions have to come after it.
.ipynb_checkpoints/
__pycache__/
logs/
work/
